# Methods and interpretation for the report and figure

## Provenance and reproduction

The only input is the analytic problem in Research objective #1, quoted in `brief.md`: f(x)=exp(x)sin(3x), x in [0,2], n=2^k for k=2,...,12. Run `python3 analysis/compute.py` from the repository root. This produces `results/errors.csv`, `results/fits.json`, and `results/validation.json`. The completed tracked run is job-188816baf1 (exit 0). Package versions and measured timings are in validation.json. No measured datasets or external literature are used.

## Exact integral and quadrature

An antiderivative is F(x)=exp(x)[sin(3x)-3cos(3x)]/10 (differentiation gives f). Thus I=F(2)-F(0)=[exp(2)(sin(6)-3cos(6))+3]/10 = -2.03488731288601525349264692566805101378089043574235... . The exact expression and 80-digit evaluation are recorded in validation.json.

With h=2/n and y_j=f(jh), T_n=h[(y_0+y_n)/2+sum_{j=1}^{n-1}y_j] and S_n=(h/3)[y_0+y_n+4 sum_{j odd}y_j+2 sum_{j even, interior}y_j]. errors.csv contains all eleven absolute errors |T_n-I| and |S_n-I|, plus signed quadrature estimates. Its column names are n,trapezoid_error,simpson_error,trapezoid_integral,simpson_integral. Node-weight sums and I use 60 decimal digits; CSV entries retain 45 significant digits.

## Fits and 95% intervals

For each rule separately, retain only E_n>1e-13 and regress y=ln(E_n) on x=ln(n) with an intercept, using unweighted ordinary least squares in binary64. The plotted fit is exp(intercept)*n**slope. With m retained points, Sxx=sum((x-xbar)^2), s²=sum(residual²)/(m-2), SE=sqrt(s²/Sxx), interval=slope +/- t_(0.975,m-2)*SE. fits.json supplies exact retained n lists, coefficients, SEs, degrees of freedom, t quantiles, residuals and R². The intervals describe the assumed linear regression model; these deterministic errors are neither independent random measurements nor exactly a power law at coarse n, so the intervals are not probabilistic bounds on the integration error or on the true asymptotic order.

