## Claims, diagnostics and comparison with theory

Both errors decrease at every refinement. The required global trapezoidal fit is close to -2, but its interval narrowly excludes -2; do not claim both expected slopes lie inside their intervals. Simpson's global slope is less negative than -4 and its interval includes -4. Coarse-grid curvature matters, especially for Simpson (local order 2.37849 on n=4 to 8). The saved log residuals show systematic departures from a straight line, so the intervals must be qualified as model-based descriptive summaries.

Sensitivity fits use subsets of the eligible n, without replacing the prescribed fits. For n>=64, slopes are -2.0000102975641902 (trapezoidal) and -3.9996402472827715 (Simpson). For n>=256, they are -2.0000011286895822 and -3.9999573557261248. Local orders log2(E_n/E_2n) approach 2 and 4; on the final refinement they are 2.000000059405823 and 3.99999840909797. These results support convergence toward the expected asymptotic orders despite finite-grid global-fit deviations.

For smooth f, the Euler–Maclaurin leading errors are T_n-I=h²[f'(2)-f'(0)]/12+O(h⁴), and S_n-I=h⁴[f'''(2)-f'''(0)]/180+O(h⁶). Here f'=exp(x)(sin(3x)+3cos(3x)) and f'''=exp(x)(-26sin(3x)-18cos(3x)); both endpoint differences are nonzero. Since h=2/n, these give exponents -2 and -4. This is an analytic explanation, not a replacement for the measured fits.

## Numerical checks

Repeating the full node-weight evaluation at 90 digits changes any error by at most 6.0187986e-48 in relative terms. An independent geometric-series evaluation for exp((1+3i)x), taking the imaginary part and using S_n=(4T_n-T_(n/2))/3, agrees with 90-digit node-weight estimates to at most 9.4352776e-88 absolute. Both checks assert tolerances in compute.py and passed.

An IEEE binary64 sensitivity calculation uses math.fsum. It selects the same fit points and gives slopes -2.001080259168367 and -3.8936379469891125. Relative error discrepancies peak at about 5.46e-10 for trapezoidal and 0.00401 for Simpson (the latter at n=4096). Even the smallest Simpson error is well resolved in the primary high-precision computation; there is no observed flattening in those primary errors. Do not claim a measured round-off plateau.

## Presentation handoff

Plot n against absolute error on log-log axes. Show all eleven points per rule, each fitted power law only across its retained domain, and the 1e-13 threshold. Expected-slope guides may be normalized at a fine-grid point but should be labeled as guides, not additional fits. Natural logs were used for fitting; log-base choice does not change slopes. Use enough interval digits to preserve the trapezoidal upper endpoint's exclusion of -2 (for example six decimal places).

